with 8k rows is seconds. Total is well under a few hours on a single node.\n\nLet me write a thorough design. First, is it worth quickly re-confirming a few environment facts for the feasibility section (core count, etc.)? I already have them. I could quickly check the number of CPUs available via os.cpu_count. Not necessary.\n\nOne thing I could actually do: verify that the tmpfs in /tmp is a good scratch location, and document the storage plan. Already have it.\n\nCompute a small table of rough estimates via mental arithmetic and present it. Or run a small shell computation with python3 for the combinatorics (no numpy needed) — trivial, unnecessary.\n\nDesign details:\n\n**Data schema (assumed, to be verified on arrival):**\n- File: a single CSV/parquet, one row per design; 26 geometry parameters + Cd (+ possibly cdA, body-family label). Verify column names on arrival; steps: drop non-parametric metadata, standardize inputs with train-only statistics.\n\n**N grid:**\n- Let N_total = number of designs in the file. Split first: train pool 70% / val 15% / test 15% (fixed seed, e.g., seed 0), at the design level (main protocol).\n- N grid: the largest power-of-2-style log grid within the train pool: N_k = round(0.7·N_total · f), f ∈ {1/32, 1/16, 1/8, 1/4, 1/2, 1}; with a floor of N_min=128. Concrete example: N_total=8,046 → pool ~5,632 → grid {176, 352, 704, 1408, 2816, 5632}. Truncation rule: if N_total < 4,000 → fewer rungs (at least 4 rungs from 128 to max) and skip the power-law fit (fallback: empirical curves + Spearman monotonicity test).\n- Nested subsets: random permutation of the train pool fixed once (seed 0); subset k = the first N_k elements of that permutation → nesting is guaranteed. Seeds re-subsample? Two options: (A) nesting via a single permutation, seeds vary model initialization; (B) resample subsets per seed to quantify data-order variance. Choose B for CI honesty, but keep nesting within a seed: for seed s, its own permutation, subsets are nested