early stopping at small N can overfit val via selection… minor; and val is fixed across N so selection bias is constant. Declare this.\n\n**Partitioning protocol:**\n- P1 (main): design-level stratified random 70/15/15 (stratified by body family when available), fixed seed 20241022.\n- P2 (sensitivity): group split by body family (family-wise leave-one-group-out test, or train on 2 families and test on the 3rd — three folds, aggregate MAE).\n- P3 (sensitivity): clustering split — k-means (k = N/20 or fixed k such that cluster sizes are ~) on standardized 26 parameters, assign whole clusters to splits at 70/15/15; additionally report pairwise distance summary (median nearest-neighbor distance in parameter space within train vs. across splits).\n- Report all three; conclusions must be robust across P1–P3, otherwise report protocol dependence.\n\n**Metrics:**\n- Primary: test-set MAE of Cd. Secondary: RMSE and MAE in percent (% of true Cd). Per body family. Calibration is not relevant.\n- Test set is touched once per (model, N, seed, protocol) after all tuning is frozen.\n\n**Statistical analysis:**\n- Per (model, N): 5 seeds → mean MAE, IC95 via percentile bootstrap of seed-level MAE (B=10,000); with 5 seeds use t-based CI (df=4) — bootstrap of 5 points is degenerate; declare: use t-intervals across seeds for per-point CIs, and bootstrap over seeds at the curve level for the contrast of differences (model A minus model B at each N, paired by seed).\n- Success criterion (pre-registered):\n  - \"Improves with data\" for model m: (i) fit a power law MAE(N)=a·N^(−α)+c by nonlinear least squares to seed-averaged curves (α>0 and α's IC95 excludes 0 via residual bootstrap B=2000), AND (ii) monotonicity: MAE(N_max) < MAE(N_min) with paired-seed IC95 of the difference excluding 0. Both required.\n  - \"Differences across architectures\": interaction — primary test: paired-seed difference curve D(N) = MAE_A(N) − MAE_B(N) for all architecture pairs (10 pairs), Holm-corrected across pairs; significant if IC95 of D excludes 0 at ≥ half the rungs, or if there is a sign crossover in the curve (A better at small N, B better at large N) with both endpoints' IC95s excluding 0. Additionally, pairwise comparison of fitted α (bootstrap CI on α differences).\n  - Threshold MAE < 0.005 on Cd: operational descriptor only — used to compute \"N at target\" per architecture, and explicitly not an external standard.\n- Fallback for N < 4k: skip the power law (too few rungs/points), report Spearman rank correlation of MAE vs. N, pairwise monotonicity contrasts, endpoint differences.\n\n**Figures/tables:**\n- F1: learning curves, 1 panel per protocol, MAE vs. N (log-x), 5 models, bands = IC95.\n- F2: power-law fits overlaid + table of α, c, R² with CIs.\n- F3: difference heatmap D(N) model×N (paired) with significance markers.\n- F4: per-family breakdown (grouped bars at N_max) + per-family curves as supplementary.\n- T1: dataset audit (rows, columns, Cd statistics, family counts). T2: frozen hyperparameters per model. T3: α/c table. T4: per-model, per-family N_at_target (operational threshold). T5: leakage audit (intra-cluster statistics, per-protocol nearest-neighbor distances).\n\n**Feasibility:**\n- Needed: (1) local data files (currently absent — hard blocker), (2) network enabled + pip install numpy scikit-learn xgboost (and, for plots, matplotlib); MLP in pure numpy to avoid heavy dependencies, or scikit-learn's MLPRegressor is also acceptable (sufficient for this scale and single-threaded per fit), (3) storage: files are on the order of MB; run artifacts in /tmp (1TB tmpfs). /workspace has only 8.9GB free — sufficient but use /tmp for scratch.\n- Rough time estimate (paper estimate, clearly flagged as an estimate): max N ≈ 6k × 26 features: ridge/KRR/RF a few seconds each; XGBoost under a minute; MLP (sklearn, 2–3k epochs, 6k samples) a few minutes per fit on 1 core. Total fits: 5 models × 6 N × 5 seeds × 3 protocols = 450 fits + tuning 5×30 = 150 val fits at max N. Estimate: MLP dominates ≈ 450×~2–5 min ≈ 15–40 core-hours; with 224 cores parallelizing over (model, N, seed) → well under 1 hour of wall clock. Overall including tuning: tens of core-hours.\n- Risks: no network → nothing executable; data absent → design only; sklearn MLPRegressor is slow → parallelize across fits.\n\nAlso mention a data-arrival audit checklist (row counts, NaNs, column names, Cd range ~0.19–0.54 typical — but don't assert as fact; mark expected values as assumptions from the public paper).\n\nI need to write the complete design and end with end_researcher_turn stage=result. Also explicitly state that nothing has been executed.\n\nFor the feasibility estimate I might do one quick sanity check that sklearn's MLPRegressor is the choice — nothing can be confirmed (not installed). Oh well.\n\nWrite it in Spanish. The answer field will be a complete design. It will be long but well structured.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 -c \"import os; print('cpus:', os.cpu_count()); import shutil; u=shutil.disk_usage('/tmp'); print('/tmp free GB:', u.free//2**30); w=shutil.disk_usage('/workspace'); print('/workspace free GB:', w.free//2**30)\"; echo '---'; python3 -c \"\n# combinatoria del diseno (aritmetica pura, sin librerias)\nmodels=5; rungs=6; seeds=5; protocols=3\nfits=models*rungs*seeds*protocols\ntune=models*30\nprint('ajustes totales (modelos*rungs*seeds*protocolos):', fits)\nprint('ajustes de tuning (5 modelos x 30 configs al max N):', tune)\nprint('total encajes:', fits+tune)\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8eabebfe2c3bda4c", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-8eabebfe2c3bda4c", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 -c \\\"import os; print('cpus:', os.cpu_count()); import shutil; u=shutil.disk_usage('/tmp'); print('/tmp free GB:', u.free//2**